# Cloning of Insulin as Two Mini-Genes


@left


![Instructor figure related to recombinant insulin production](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_065.jpg)


@right

- A- and B-chain genes cloned separately
- Each chain fused to beta-galactosidase for purification
- Separate plasmids expressed in bacteria
- Fusion proteins purified; cyanogen bromide cleavage
- A- and B-chains isolated
- Chains refolded; disulfide bonds restored
=== layout: two-panels



@left


![Instructor figure related to recombinant insulin production](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_066.jpg)


@right


- Insulin is produced and stored in the body as a hexamer, while the active form is the monomer.
- High concentrations of insulin cause the monomers to clump into hexamers.
- Changing the proline to aspartic acid prevents recombinant insulin from clumping.
