=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Toxic Effects of Protein Over Production


@left

- Excess expression -> misfolding and inclusion bodies
- Regulate induction: pET + IPTG; pBAD + arabinose
- Slow production; support folding with chaperones
- Lower temperature; improve solubility
- Refold purified protein when needed
- Improve sequence and cellular stability

@top-right


![Student-created diagram contrasting normal and overloaded protein expression.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_overproduction-and-inclusion-bodies.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created overview of overproduction, inclusion bodies, and refolding.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_bacterial-protein-refolding.jpg)
=== layout: big-image



@image


![Instructor figure related to bacterial protein expression and folding](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_067.jpg)


@caption


Bacterial Protein Expression And Folding
=== layout: two-panels


# N-terminal amino acid


@left


- The identity of the N-terminal amino acid greatly affects the half-life of proteins.
- Although all polypeptide chains are made with methionine as the initial amino acid, many proteins are processed later.
- Therefore the N-terminal amino acid of mature proteins varies greatly.


@right


![Instructor figure related to bacterial protein expression and folding](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_068.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# PEST sequences


@left


- The presence of certain internal sequences greatly destabilizes proteins.
- PEST sequences are regions of 10 to 60 amino acids that are rich in
  - P (proline), E (glutamate), S (serine), and T (threonine).
- The PEST sequences create domains whose structures are recognized by proteolytic enzymes.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to bacterial protein expression and folding](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_068.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to bacterial protein expression and folding](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_069.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Molecular Chaperones Help Prevent Inclusion Body Formation


@left


![Instructor figure related to bacterial protein expression and folding](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_069.png)


@right


- If recombinant proteins fold aberrantly, they aggregate and form inclusion bodies.
- If, during translation, molecular chaperone proteins hold the recombinant protein, the new polypeptide will fold properly and become fully functional.
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


